roomlerd for Debian/Ubuntu
==========================

The daemon binary is /usr/bin/meshharbord.

Which unit do I want?
---------------------

The package ships TWO units. Pick ONE — running both enrolls the same host
twice and they fight over the same identity.

  meshharbor.service   (systemd --user, runs as YOU)   -> desktop host
  meshharbord.service  (systemd system, runs as root)  -> headless / server / WSL

                            | meshharbor.service | meshharbord.service
  --------------------------+-----------------+------------------
  screen capture + input    | yes             | the person on screen, else opt-in
  overlay mesh (roomler0)   | no              | yes (TUN needs CAP_NET_ADMIN)
  unattended self-update    | yes (helper)    | yes
  starts before login       | no              | yes

Want remote desktop? Use the user unit. Want a mesh node that runs with
nobody logged in? Use the system unit.

Desktop host (systemd --user)
-----------------------------

  # 1. Generate an enrollment JWT at https://roomler.ai/ (Admin -> Agents).
  # 2. Enroll as your normal user, NOT root:
  meshharbord enroll --server https://roomler.ai \
                  --token <paste-enrollment-jwt> \
                  --name "$(hostname)"

  # 3. Optional smoke test — needs an active graphical session (X11, or
  #    Wayland since 0.4.130); a tty login won't capture anything.
  RUST_LOG=roomlerd=debug,webrtc=info meshharbord run

  # 4. Updates (FR-96): this daemon runs as you and cannot write /usr, so a
  #    ROOT helper installs them for it. Installing this package turned it on
  #    (meshharbor-update.timer + .path); check with:
  systemctl is-enabled meshharbor-update.timer meshharbor-update.path

  systemctl --user daemon-reload
  systemctl --user enable --now meshharbor.service
  journalctl --user -u meshharbor.service -f

  # A headless host additionally needs lingering, or the user manager
  # (and the agent with it) exits when you log out:
  sudo loginctl enable-linger "$USER"

Self-update note: with the helper on (step 4), each update installs
unattended as root (`journalctl -u meshharbor-update.service`), and the daemon
restarts onto the new binary once nothing it serves would be cut. Opt out
with `sudo touch /etc/meshharbor/disable-auto-update`. If you disable the helper
(`sudo systemctl disable --now meshharbor-update.timer meshharbor-update.path`), a
reinstall or an upgrade of this package leaves it disabled. Without the
helper the daemon installs nothing: its unit's NoNewPrivileges stops pkexec
and sudo. It logs the command that turns updates on, and hands its updates
to the helper as soon as it is on, with no restart. A daemon from before
0.4.137 does not restart onto a new binary by itself: after installing this
package over one, log out and back in, or run
`systemctl --user restart meshharbor.service`.

Headless / server / WSL (systemd system)
----------------------------------------

Config lives at a machine-global path, not in a home directory, because the
unit starts at boot before any login. Enroll against the SAME path you run
with — `enroll` creates the directory and writes the file 0600 (it holds a
bearer token):

  sudo roomlerd --config /etc/meshharbor/config.toml \
       enroll --server https://roomler.ai \
              --token <paste-enrollment-jwt> \
              --name "$(hostname)"

  sudo systemctl daemon-reload
  sudo systemctl enable --now meshharbord.service
  sudo journalctl -u meshharbord.service -f

For a different config path, drop MESHHARBORD_CONFIG=/path/to/config.toml into
/etc/default/meshharbord.

With the system unit, the root daemon installs its own updates. The update
helper this package turned on (meshharbor-update.timer + .path) sees
meshharbord.service enabled and steps aside, so two updaters never race;
`install.sh --system` turns it off outright.

Both flows above are what `install.sh --role daemon [--system]` automates.

Enabling the overlay mesh
-------------------------

Set `overlay_enabled = true` at the TOP LEVEL of config.toml — it is a
root-level key, so do not append it at the end of the file, where it would
land inside a [table] — then restart the daemon. The node generates and
persists its WireGuard key on the first overlay-enabled start.

The OS TUN needs CAP_NET_ADMIN, so this works under meshharbord.service (root)
and not under meshharbor.service. On a host that cannot run as root, set
ROOMLERD_OVERLAY_NETSTACK_SOCKS=<port> for the userspace netstack
instead: outbound mesh access through a loopback SOCKS5 proxy, no TUN and no
routes — but other nodes cannot reach services on this host.

Wayland
-------

Under the user unit, a Wayland session captures itself:

  GNOME   mutter's own screencast, from 0.4.130 (input from 0.4.132). It
          asks nobody. While the screen is locked GNOME shares nothing, and
          the viewer says to unlock it at the computer.
  KDE     the desktop portal, from 0.4.132 (input from 0.4.133). It asks
  others  the person at the screen once (KDE's "Remote Control" dialog),
          then remembers the answer.

The system unit switches capture on by itself for the person at the screen,
Wayland or X11, when all of these hold:

  - no capture key is set;
  - the seat shows that person's own session, never the login screen;
  - that person can be told: the desktop companion runs for them, or their
    notifications show buttons, so they see "This screen is being viewed"
    with Disconnect;
  - on X11, the desktop reports its lock (GNOME Shell or KDE).

It then reads the screen below the desktop and injects input there, bound to
that person's session. wayland_auto_system / x11_auto_system = false switch
it off. Anything else (the login screen, a desktop that cannot tell anyone)
needs both of these keys, which read the screen and inject input into whatever
has focus, the login screen included, so nothing sets them for you:

  sudo meshharbor config set drm_capture true
  sudo meshharbor config set uinput true
  sudo systemctl restart meshharbord.service

Until then the viewer names what is missing and these two commands. It never
shows a black screen or calls a desktop host headless.

What each desktop, install and session state gives, and every message the
viewer can show: docs/wayland.md in the roomler-ai repository.

